---
worker-id: ${WORKER_ID}
worker-description: "docker_worker_${WORKER_ID}"
broker-uri: "${BROKER_URI}"
headers:
    env:
        - bash
        - c-gcc-linux
        - cxx-gcc-linux
        - python3
        - cs-dotnet-core
        - java
        # Data-only: the worker runs no toolchain for this one, it runs the exercise author's own
        # judge against whatever the student uploaded. It still has to be advertised here or the
        # broker finds no worker for the environment and every such submission becomes a failure.
        - data-linux
    threads: 1
hwgroup: "${WORKER_HWGROUP}"
working-directory: "/var/recodex-worker-wd"
file-managers:
    # The internal address, not the public one: this is the worker talking to the core over the
    # compose network, and the public domain need not resolve here at all.
    - hostname: "${API_INTERNAL_ADDRESS}"
      username: "${WORKER_FILES_AUTH_USER}"
      password: "${WORKER_FILES_AUTH_PASSWORD}"
file-cache:
    cache-dir: "/var/recodex-worker-cache"
logger:
    file: "/var/log/recodex/worker"
    level: "${LOG_LEVEL}"
    max-size: 1048576
    rotations: 3
limits:
    time: 30
    wall-time: 30
    extra-time: 2
    stack-size: 0
    memory: 1048576
    extra-memory: 64
    parallel: 0
    disk-quotas: false
    disk-size: 1048576
    disk-files: 100
    environ-variable:
        HOME: /box
        # **/usr/local/bin comes first, and Python is why.** This image builds Python 3.13 from
        # source into /usr/local (Debian 12 ships only 3.11), so `python3` exists at
        # /usr/local/bin/python3 and *not* at /usr/bin/python3. isolate's default rules bind /usr
        # into the sandbox, so the interpreter is visible there -- it was simply not on this PATH,
        # and every Python submission died with "Exited with error status 127". That went unnoticed
        # because the sandbox could not start at all on a cgroup v2 host until isolate 2.7, so no
        # submission ever got far enough to fail this way.
        PATH: /usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin
        # .NET writes a first-run marker, a telemetry sentinel and a NuGet fallback folder under
        # $HOME the first time the CLI runs. Inside isolate, $HOME is the per-submission /box,
        # which is wiped between runs -- so without these every single C# submission would pay
        # for (and could fail on) that first-run setup. Set here rather than in the pipeline so
        # it applies to compile and execute alike.
        DOTNET_CLI_TELEMETRY_OPTOUT: 1
        DOTNET_SKIP_FIRST_TIME_EXPERIENCE: 1
        DOTNET_NOLOGO: 1
        DOTNET_CLI_HOME: /box
    bound-directories:
        - dst: "/tmp"
          mode: "tmp"
        # **/opt, read-only, and C# is why.** isolate's default rules bind /usr, /bin and /lib
        # into the sandbox and nothing else, so a toolchain installed under /opt is invisible
        # there. The .NET SDK is: `/usr/bin/dotnet` is a symlink to `/opt/dotnet/dotnet`, which
        # inside the sandbox is a link to nowhere -- every C# submission died with
        # `execve("/usr/bin/dotnet"): No such file or directory`, which reads like a missing
        # toolchain and is a missing *mount*. The job configuration compounds the confusion by
        # naming `/opt/dotnet/sdk/latest/Roslyn/bincore/csc.dll` and the shared runtime under
        # /opt as well, so nothing about the C# pipelines works without this line.
        #
        # Same family as the PATH entry above: the sandbox's view of the filesystem is narrower
        # than the container's, and anything this image installs outside /usr has to be said out
        # loud. No `mode`, which means read-only -- the worker's own default for a bound directory
        # (`helpers/config.cpp`: `dir_perm::RO` unless a mode says otherwise), and a submission has
        # no business writing to a toolchain.
        - src: "/opt"
          dst: "/opt"
        # **/etc/java-17-openjdk, and Java is the same story told by Debian instead.** The JDK's
        # `conf/` is a directory of symlinks into /etc -- `java.security` among them -- and the
        # worker binds only `/etc/alternatives`, so `javac` starts and then dies with
        # `java.lang.InternalError: Error loading java.security file`. Which looks like a broken
        # JDK and is, again, a missing mount.
        #
        # **The version is in the path**, and nothing else in this stack names it: the Dockerfile
        # installs `default-jdk` precisely so it does not have to, and on Debian 12 that resolves
        # to OpenJDK 17. So a Debian base image that moves its default JDK moves this path with it,
        # and the symptom if this line is not moved too is that one error above. There is no
        # versionless alias under /etc to point at instead.
        - src: "/etc/java-17-openjdk"
          dst: "/etc/java-17-openjdk"
max-output-length: 4096
max-carboncopy-length: 1048576
cleanup-submission: false
...
